                "username": "fredchenquanta"
            },
            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/assert-rmc-main-power-enable",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Should we really be automatically turning on the node just because the RMC says it is _ok_ to turn on power?  Don't we have some other criteria for that?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/assert-rmc-main-power-enable",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I guess we already do this on Catalina also?  Can you confirm?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/assert-rmc-main-power-enable",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Fred Chen",
                        "email": "Fred.Chen@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "fredchenquanta"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi Patrick, after checking with Catalina, they also auto-power-on once the GPIO is asserted. Their power-on command first reads the RMC-enable GPIO pin on the IO expander via an I2C raw command to determine whether the system is allowed to power on.\nSince our design uses SGPIO, it\u2019s difficult to follow the same approach, but our CPLD will check both the tray-leak status and the RMC-enable signal before starting the power sequence. Is this design acceptable, or do you recommend adding any further check conditions in our power-on flow?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/assert-rmc-main-power-enable",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/assert-rmc-main-power-enable",
                    "line": 3,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Can you please format this so it isn't 200 characters wide?  You can run `beautysh`."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/assert-rmc-main-power-enable",
                    "line": 3,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Fred Chen",
